//? Title: Connected Components: From Provisional IDs to Measured Blobs
//? Subtitle: Actual two-pass union-find decisions, four versus eight neighbours and checked native image summaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a rectangular image of foreground and background cells, assign the same
positive integer to exactly those foreground cells joined by a path of permitted
neighbours. This turns a mask into separate regions that can be counted and
measured, but diagonal contact makes the connectivity choice consequential.
This report follows actual provisional labels and union decisions, compares
four- and eight-neighbour masks, reproduces the original four rectangles and
measures their areas, centroids, bounds and grid perimeters. Balanced native
union-find supports the complexity claim; exhaustive independent flood-fill
checks test the resulting partitions.
)

\toc

> The method: connected components of a pixel graph

Treat each nonzero input cell as a vertex. Under four-connectivity, two vertices
are adjacent exactly when their row/column indices differ by a unit orthogonal
step; eight-connectivity also permits the four diagonals. A component is a maximal
set of vertices joined by paths. Background cells keep label zero. All nonzero
integer values have the same foreground meaning, including negative values:
this API does not perform an intensity threshold or same-colour segmentation.

The native algorithm scans rows from top to bottom and columns from left to
right. Only earlier neighbours are needed: up and left for four-connectivity,
plus upper-left and upper-right for eight-connectivity. An upper-right cell is
already visited because its entire row precedes the current row.

At a foreground cell, create a fresh provisional label if no earlier neighbour
has a label. Otherwise write the minimum of the earlier provisional labels and
union its set with every admitted neighbour label. Actual attempts include
self-unions and duplicate labels; only unions between distinct roots merge sets.
A second row-major scan resolves representatives and gives each new component
its next dense ID, starting at one. The final ID follows first component occurrence,
not the numeric value or rank of its internal representative.

> A bridge that really merges two provisional sets

The mask has first row (1,0,1) and second row (1,1,1). Initially its top foreground
cells create IDs 1 and 2. Scanning the bottom-right cell admits up=2 and left=1;
its assigned minimum is 1, and the union connects the two sets. The two-pass
figure and tables come from one native execution, with no prefix reruns.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/component_bridge.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::connected_components_trace({{1,0,1},{1,1,1}});
  dm::graphics::diagram_label_images({{t.provisional,"Pass 1: provisional IDs"},
    {t.analysis.result.labels,"Pass 2: dense IDs"}}).save_svg("build/component_bridge.svg");
  std::print("| foreground cell (row,col) | earlier provisional labels | assigned | new set | union record range |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& cell:t.cells) {
    std::string labels;
    for(std::size_t k=0;k<cell.earlier_count;++k) labels+=std::format("{}{}",k?", ":"",cell.earlier_labels[k]);
    std::print("| ({},{}) | {} | {} | {} | [{},{}) |\n",cell.row,cell.col,labels.empty()?"none":labels,cell.assigned,cell.created,cell.union_begin,cell.union_end);
  }
  std::print("\n| union record | operands | actual roots before | merged | winner | attached root |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.unions.size();++k) {
    const auto& u=t.unions[k];
    std::print("| {} | {}, {} | {}, {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,u.a,u.b,u.root_a,u.root_b,u.merged,u.winner,u.loser);
  }
  const auto& w=t.analysis.work;
  std::print("\n{} provisional sets - {} successful union = {} final component. {} foreground cells; {} union attempts; {} find calls.\n",w.labels_created,w.successful_unions,t.analysis.result.count,w.foreground,w.union_calls,w.find_calls);
}
```
