\image(figures/connected_components.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original four regions have areas 9, 8, 8 and 16. This native figure draws actual square cells with numerical IDs, rather than unscaled scatter markers.)
\alttext(A twelve-by-twelve grid shows four separated rectangles: a three-by-three top-left region, a two-by-four top-right region, a four-by-two bottom-left region and a four-by-four bottom-right region.)

An a-by-b rectangle has area ab, grid perimeter 2(a+b) and centroid halfway
between each inclusive pair of bounds. The table checks all three formulas.
Each rectangle creates just one provisional set, so this original example has
zero successful unions. Its many redundant union attempts still perform real
work. The bridge example supplies the equivalence merge that the original
four-rectangle illustration did not actually exercise.

> Checking counts, connectivity and operation bounds

A rows-by-cols checkerboard beginning with foreground at (0,0) has
F=ceil(rows·cols/2) foreground cells. Four-connectivity makes every cell a
singleton. If both dimensions are at least two, eight-connectivity joins them
all: diagonal steps connect successive rows, and alternating cells in a row
connect through the neighbouring row. A single row or column has no usable
diagonals and keeps F components. The distinction matters for degenerate images.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/component_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> side,probes4,probes8;
  std::print("| square side | cells | foreground | four-neighbour count | eight-neighbour count | eight provisional sets | successful unions | eight union attempts | eight halving steps |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t n:{8,16,32,64,128}) {
    const auto image=dm::algorithms::component_checkerboard(n,n);
    const auto four=dm::algorithms::connected_components_analyze(image);
    const auto eight=dm::algorithms::connected_components_analyze(image,true);
    const auto& w=eight.work;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,n*n,w.foreground,four.result.count,eight.result.count,w.labels_created,w.successful_unions,w.union_calls,w.find_steps);
    side.push_back(n);probes4.push_back(double(four.work.neighbor_probes)/(n*n));
    probes8.push_back(double(w.neighbor_probes)/(n*n));
    if(four.result.count!=int((n*n+1)/2) || eight.result.count!=1) return 1;
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(side,probes4,"four neighbours",{37,99,235},2).points(side,probes4,"",{37,99,235},5)
      .line(side,probes8,"eight neighbours",{220,38,38},2).points(side,probes8,"",{220,38,38},5)
      .legend({"four neighbours","eight neighbours"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Checkerboard: bounded earlier-neighbour work")
      .x_label("side n of an n by n mask").y_label("actual probes divided by all n squared cells")
      .y_limits(0,2.6).size(900,560).save_svg("build/component_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| square side | cells | foreground | four-neighbour count | eight-neighbour count | eight provisional sets | successful unions | eight union attempts | eight halving steps |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 64 | 32 | 32 | 1 | 4 | 3 | 49 | 45 |
| 16 | 256 | 128 | 128 | 1 | 8 | 7 | 225 | 245 |
| 32 | 1024 | 512 | 512 | 1 | 16 | 15 | 961 | 1125 |
| 64 | 4096 | 2048 | 2048 | 1 | 32 | 31 | 3969 | 4805 |
| 128 | 16384 | 8192 | 8192 | 1 | 64 | 63 | 16129 | 19845 |
\image(figures/component_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The counters measure actual in-bounds predecessor inspections, including background neighbours of foreground cells. They stay bounded per input cell. This plot does not estimate an inverse-Ackermann factor or compare wall-clock implementations.)
\alttext(Blue four-neighbour probes per cell approach one and red eight-neighbour probes per cell approach two as checkerboard side length increases.)

For N cells and F foreground cells, both scans visit N positions. Each foreground
cell inspects at most two or four earlier positions and attempts at most that
many unions. If U is the actual union-call count, there are exactly 2U+F find
calls: two per attempted union and one per foreground cell during remapping.
There are P≤F provisional sets, and each successful union decreases their number
by one; the final count is P minus successful unions.

Balanced union with halving gives O(N α(N)) scan/forest work, plus O(R) shape
validation and row storage for R rows. The explicit O(R+N α(N)) bound also covers
valid zero-width images. Output and forest storage are O(R+N); optional actual
history remains O(R+N) because it has F cell records, U≤4F union records and
owned maps/forest tables. The legacy result-only and analysis paths do not
allocate those histories. Measurements need O(R+N+count) work and O(count)
additional storage. No external dataset is needed for these exact geometric and
combinatorial checks.

> Validation, limits and measurement semantics

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::algorithms;
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> bridge{{1,0,1},{1,1,1}};
  const auto exact=connected_components_trace(bridge,false,{6,9});
  std::print("Exact caps: {} cells, {} actual history records; {} component.\n",exact.analysis.work.validated_cells,exact.cells.size()+exact.unions.size(),exact.analysis.result.count);
  try { (void)connected_components_trace(bridge,false,{6,8});return 1; }
  catch(const std::length_error&) {std::print("One-below event cap rejects the nine-record history.\n");}
  try { (void)connected_components_trace({{},{1}},false,{1,1});return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {std::print("Ragged rows are rejected even after an empty first row.\n");}
  const auto empty=connected_components(std::vector<std::vector<int>>(3));
  std::print("A three-row zero-width image retains {} rows and {} components.\n",empty.labels.size(),empty.count);
  const auto external=connected_component_stats(ConnectedComponents{{{1,0,1}},1});
  std::print("An externally supplied disconnected ID is measured as one group: area {}, grid perimeter {}.\n",external[0].area,external[0].perimeter4);
  try { (void)connected_component_stats(ConnectedComponents{{{1,3,3}},3});return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {std::print("Missing dense IDs are rejected by measurement.\n");}
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact caps: 6 cells, 9 actual history records; 1 component.
One-below event cap rejects the nine-record history.
Ragged rows are rejected even after an empty first row.
A three-row zero-width image retains 3 rows and 0 components.
An externally supplied disconnected ID is measured as one group: area 2, grid perimeter 8.
Missing dense IDs are rejected by measurement.
// result_end

Shape validation covers every row before result allocation or cell caps. A
rectangular zero-width image is valid; a later nonempty row after an empty first
row is ragged and throws. All image indices use size_t. Positive provisional
IDs remain int for compatibility, with an explicit INT_MAX exhaustion check.
The original result fields and deterministic row-major dense-ID order remain.

Positive max_cells is a complete-image preflight. max_events counts actual
foreground-cell plus union-attempt records online; background does not consume
history entries. Zero disables either optional cap, and analysis ignores the
history cap. Failure returns no partial result or input mutation. Work counters
are checked rather than silently wrapping. Every trace owns its maps, decisions
and final forest; colouring performs no extra union/find operations.

Measurement validates shape, label ranges, count and complete dense-ID presence.
It summarises supplied labels without proving their connectivity. Passing a
produced labelling gives connected regions by the algorithm's contract; passing
an externally assembled map groups whichever cells share an ID. Colours cycle
after eight positive IDs in the grid renderer, so numerical IDs remain the
identity when a figure contains more regions than colours.

> Conclusion

The bridge exposes the actual provisional-set merge; diagonal contact shows why
four- and eight-connectivity answer different questions. The original rectangles
retain their four areas and now have verified centroids, bounds and exposed-edge
measurements. Balanced native union-find, checked shapes, owned execution records
and exact operation counts make the complexity and results inspectable.

> References

- A. Rosenfeld and J. L. Pfaltz (1966), *Sequential Operations in Digital Picture Processing*, Journal of the ACM 13(4), 471–494. [Original paper hosted by the author](https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~jlp/66.sequential.op.pdf), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/321356.321357). The current implementation uses a balanced union-find representation of provisional equivalences.
- R. E. Tarjan (1975), *Efficiency of a Good But Not Linear Set Union Algorithm*, Journal of the ACM 22(2), 215–225. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/321879.321884).
- R. E. Tarjan and J. van Leeuwen (1984), *Worst-Case Analysis of Set Union Algorithms*, Journal of the ACM 31(2), 245–281. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/62.2160). The balanced rank-plus-halving variant supports the stated amortised bound; actual histories and measurements are native implementation additions.
